        "propositionDetails": [
          {
            "tone": "relational",
            "badge": "P1",
            "title": "P1 — Multidimensional reconfiguration of perceived value",
            "text": "The integration of AI into consulting missions reconfigures client-perceived value across three interdependent dimensions: relational (R, the evaluation of legitimacy, maintenance of authority and trust), processual (P, the orchestration of co-creation), and cognitive (C, divided into C1 cognitive appropriation and C2 creation of new managerial knowledge).\n\nAI does not reconfigure consulting perceived value uniformly. Its effect on each dimension depends on how it is introduced and how the client interprets it. The same integration of AI can amplify perceived value in one configuration and erode it in another. AI reconfigures each link contingently, by amplifying or destabilizing it, according to P2 and P3.\n\nDimensions of consulting perceived value in the AI era: the R - P - C triangle.\n\nThe framework adopts an integrated processual approach, consistent with the perspective of human-AI entanglement (Kaartemo & Helkkula, 2025) and with Service-Dominant Logic (Grönroos & Voima, 2013; S. L. Vargo & Lusch, 2016): value emerges in action, through dynamic interdependencies between R, P and C.\n\nThis choice opens a space that quantitative SEM-type approaches, such as Oesterle et al. (2020), do not seek to occupy. Hence the choice of a qualitative design articulated around the R-P-C triangle.\n\nR concerns the legitimacy of the consulting service, actively produced through interaction and tested by the opacity of human-AI systems.\n\nP concerns the experience of co-creation itself: its nature, readability and distribution of roles, reconfigured by AI as a third entangled actor in the consultant-client-AI relationship.\n\nC1 concerns cognitive appropriation: the ability to interpret, understand and evaluate what has been co-produced through consulting, so that mission outputs become intelligible, assessable, appropriable and usable as managerial knowledge.\n\nC2 concerns the creation of explicit and especially tacit managerial knowledge, in the sense of know-what and know-how (Ciampi, 2017; Harfouche et al., 2023).\n\nThree links are demonstrated in the literature to varying degrees: R ↔ P, P ↔ C, and R ↔ C. The original contribution of this research lies in theorizing the dynamic reconfiguration of perceived value: the way AI amplifies, destabilizes or reverses each of these interdependencies contingently, under the effect of substantive transparency (P2) and moderating conditions (P3)."
          },
          {
            "tone": "processual",
            "badge": "P2",
            "title": "P2 — Transparency as the mediating mechanism",
            "text": "Transparency is the mediating mechanism of consulting perceived value in the AI era. Transparency is understood here as the intelligibility of the AI-assisted system through its observable effects (Ananny & Crawford, 2018).\n\nFollowing Ananny & Crawford (2018), who underline the limits of pure algorithmic transparency, we distinguish performative transparency from substantive transparency.\n\nPerformative transparency means disclosing the use of AI. Substantive transparency, understood as a translation of meaning, is different: the issue for consulting is not to make AI itself transparent, since it remains a black box, but to justify politically and technically why the AI-assisted result is valid for this client, thereby enabling its conversion into managerial knowledge that can be appropriated and mobilized by the client.\n\nP2 therefore posits that substantive transparency weighs more heavily on consulting perceived value in the AI era because it supports client appropriation and, ultimately, perceived value, depending on the conditions specified in P3."
          },
          {
            "tone": "cognitive",
            "badge": "P3",
            "title": "P3 — Actor-level contingencies of substantive transparency",
            "text": "AI literacy and algorithm aversion.\n\nThree categories of contingency determine whether P2 does or does not produce attribution, appropriation and creation of managerial knowledge: relational contingencies (prior reputation, perceived algorithm credibility), situational contingencies (mission phase and political stakes), and actor-level contingencies (AI literacy and algorithm aversion).\n\nRelational and situational conditions are not specific to AI; they belong more broadly to the ordinary functioning of consulting missions. By contrast, actor-level contingencies are directly linked to the new phenomenon this research seeks to isolate: the client’s ability to understand and accept a production emerging from a human-AI system, and thereby to influence the perceived value of consulting.\n\nAI literacy and algorithm aversion are distinct. A client may be AI-literate while still showing aversion to AI. AI literacy determines whether the client can follow the consultant’s explanation, distinguish the part played by AI and understand the limits of what has been produced. Algorithm aversion determines whether these same contributions, even when understood, are accepted as legitimate inputs to managerial judgment. The risk is not only aversion, but also excessive trust in AI at the expense of consulting expertise.\n\nSubstantive transparency can therefore fail in two ways. It can fail cognitively when the explanation does not allow the client to understand the production chain of the result: data used, role of AI, human arbitration, validation, contextualization and managerial translation. It can also fail normatively when that chain is understood but the value of the service is attributed in an unbalanced way, either because AI use is disqualified as illegitimate, or because AI is perceived as the main source of value, relegating consulting to the provision of algorithmic outputs.\n\nValue co-destruction is the boundary condition when P2 and P3 fail simultaneously. In this framework, correct attribution is treated as an important observable condition of client appropriation and, through appropriation, as one pathway by which substantive transparency acts on perceived value."
          }
        ]
